course: dictionary
lesson: 3482
title: Familiar words and familiar faces
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Familiar means well known to you, because you have seen or heard it often. «On the high road he sees a speck that turned into a dot and then into a blob, and then into something very familiar.» The blob slowly becomes something he already knows, so familiar means known and recognised.
explain: You can also be familiar with something, which means you know about it. «Become familiar with the times tables» means learn them well until they feel known to you. Far ahead, «a glint of the familiar old river» shows a river the travellers have seen before.
explain: Familiar can also mean friendly and informal, sometimes too friendly. If someone acts very familiar with you, or asks you to keep a secret, check how it makes you feel. Even a familiar adult should not ask you to keep secrets that make you uneasy. Tell a trusted adult.
fact: Familiar means well known to you because you have seen or heard it often.
fact: If you are familiar with something, you know about it.
fact: Familiar can mean friendly and informal, sometimes too friendly.
fact: In the dark the brain fills unclear shapes with familiar things, like faces; seeing faces in objects is called pareidolia, and a night light helps.
fact: Useful phrases: «sound familiar», «look familiar», «a familiar face».
exercise: «On the high road he sees a speck that turned into something very familiar.» What does familiar mean here? | Very large and heavy | Far away and hidden | Sad and grey | *Well known to him
exercise: Sam says, «That song sounds familiar.» What does he mean? | The song is too quiet to hear | The song is very long | *He has heard the song before | The song is brand new and he has never heard it
exercise: «Far ahead they saw a glint of the familiar old river.» Find and copy the word that means well known. | ahead | glint | *familiar | river
test: If you are ___ with the times tables, you know them well. | frightened | sleepy | curious | *familiar
test: A familiar adult asks you to keep a secret that makes you uneasy. What should you do? | Tell only the person who asked you | Do nothing and forget it | *Tell a trusted adult about it | Keep the secret, because they know you well
exam: In the dark, a brain may see a face in a shape because it fills unclear shapes with what is familiar. What is this called? | Context | *Pareidolia | Dreich | Synonym
